Asynchronous sends can outrun the receiver. Bound fan-out against scarce resources and make the producer participate in the flow-control policy.

Book basis: [B3](/lore/durability-bibliography#b3-designing-elixir-systems-with-otp), [B4](/lore/durability-bibliography#b4-release-it).

## Failure it prevents

A bounded consumer hides an ever-growing upstream mailbox or set of waiting producers.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

`Queue.bounded` suspends offers at capacity. `Queue.dropping` and `Queue.sliding` choose loss policies. Bounded `Effect.forEach` and `Semaphore` cap active work. Upstream backlog needs its own bound.

Source: `packages/effect/src/Queue.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Effect.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Semaphore.ts`.

## How to check

Run a faster producer against a slower consumer. Check backlog and memory, plus the documented wait, reject, or drop behavior. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Admission control protects recovery](/lore/admission-controls-new-work), [Bulkheads isolate scarce capacity](/lore/bulkheads-isolate-capacity).

